# FIRESTORE SCHEMA GUARDIAN: INFINITY PROTOCOL v8.4.0

> Firestore is the backbone. Schema mistakes are permanent and costly. Model once, correctly.

## 1. DOCUMENT MODELING MANDATES (The Standard)

```typescript
// ✅ Canonical document shape — ALL Firestore docs MUST extend this
import { FieldValue, Timestamp } from 'firebase-admin/firestore'; // or firebase/firestore

export interface IFirestoreMetaData {
  createdAt: Timestamp;    // ALWAYS server timestamp
  updatedAt: Timestamp;    // ALWAYS server timestamp
  createdBy: string;       // uid
  isDeleted: boolean;      // Soft-delete flag (default: false)
  version: number;         // Schema version for migrations (start at 1)
}

export interface IUserProfile {
  id: string; // Document ID
  email: string;
  displayName: string;
  metadata: IFirestoreMetaData;
}

// ✅ UTILITY: Create Standard Metadata
export const createFirestoreMetadata = (uid: string) => ({
  createdAt: FieldValue.serverTimestamp(),
  updatedAt: FieldValue.serverTimestamp(),
  createdBy: uid,
  isDeleted: false,
  version: 1,
});
```

- No arrays of objects for frequently mutated data → use subcollections.
- Document size limit: **1MB hard cap** — never embed full content bodies inline.
- Timestamps: ALWAYS `FieldValue.serverTimestamp()`. Never `new Date()` or `Date.now()`.

## 2. COLLECTION ARCHITECTURE & DENORMALIZATION

| Pattern | Use When | Avoid When |
|---|---|---|
| Root collection | Independent entities (users, orders) | Deeply nested relationships |
| Subcollection | Entity has many owned children (user/orders) | Data needed across parents (use Collection Group if needed) |
| Denormalization | Read-heavy, write-rare data | High write frequency data |
| `_cache/` doc | Aggregations (counts, totals) | — |

```typescript
// ✅ FLATTENING UTILITY: Dot-notation updates for nested fields
// Prevents overwriting entire objects when updating a single nested field
export const flattenObject = (obj: any, parentKey = ''): Record<string, any> => {
  return Object.keys(obj).reduce((acc, key) => {
    const pre = parentKey.length ? parentKey + '.' : '';
    if (typeof obj[key] === 'object' && obj[key] !== null && !Array.isArray(obj[key]) && !(obj[key] instanceof Date) && !(obj[key]?.toDate)) {
      Object.assign(acc, flattenObject(obj[key], pre + key));
    } else {
      acc[pre + key] = obj[key];
    }
    return acc;
  }, {} as Record<string, any>);
};

// Usage:
// await db.collection('users').doc(id).update(flattenObject({ metadata: { updatedAt: FieldValue.serverTimestamp() } }));
```

## 3. QUERY & INDEX DISCIPLINE

- Composite indexes go in `firestore.indexes.json` — NEVER created ad-hoc in console.
- Avoid `array-contains-any` + `in` in same query (not supported).
- Pagination: ALWAYS use `startAfter(lastDoc)` cursor — never `offset()`.
- Default query limit: **25 docs**. Max: **500**. Never unbounded `.get()` on large collections.

## 4. COST CONTROL (Aggregation Pattern)

```typescript
// ✅ Aggregation via cached counter (NOT counting documents on every read)
export const getCachedUserCount = async (db: admin.firestore.Firestore): Promise<number> => {
  const cache = await db.doc('_cache/analytics').get();
  return cache.data()?.totalUsers ?? 0;
};

// ❌ EXPENSIVE — reads every document to count
// const count = (await db.collection('users').get()).size;
```

- Count operations: use `_cache/` aggregation docs updated by Cloud Function triggers (`onCreate` / `onDelete`).
- Batch writes: max **500 operations** per `writeBatch()`.
- Firestore read budget alerts: set GCP budget alert at **$5 increments**.

## 5. SECURITY RULES SOVEREIGNTY

```javascript
// ✅ Required pattern — strict type checking and timestamp validation
rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    
    function isAuth() { return request.auth != null; }
    function isOwner(uid) { return isAuth() && request.auth.uid == uid; }
    function isNotDeleted() { return !resource.data.metadata.isDeleted; }

    match /users/{userId} {
      allow read: if isOwner(userId) && isNotDeleted();
      allow create: if isOwner(userId) 
        && request.resource.data.metadata.createdAt == request.time
        && request.resource.data.metadata.isDeleted == false;
      allow update: if isOwner(userId)
        && request.resource.data.metadata.isDeleted == false
        && request.resource.data.metadata.updatedAt == request.time;
      // DELETES ARE FORBIDDEN ON THE CLIENT. Use Cloud Functions for Soft Deletes.
      allow delete: if false; 
    }
  }
}
```

- Rules MUST validate: data types, required fields, value ranges, and `isDeleted == false` on creates.
- Test rules with Firebase Emulator. Never deploy untested rules to production.

## 6. RUNTIME DATA SAFETY & PARSING

Firestore reads from the client are inherently untyped network boundaries.

- **Date Safety**: Mandate wrapping Firestore timestamps in `formatDateSafe()` before calling `.toLocaleDateString()` or plotting on UI. Failure to anticipate `null` or `undefined` timestamps leads to instant crashing.
- **Null-Safe Strings**: Mandate the use of `safeString()`, `safeLower()`, or `safeTrimLower()` when processing user/API strings to avoid `.toLowerCase() is not a function` errors. Never blindly call string methods on unvalidated data.

